# The Wheel Turns!

At the beginning of the seventeenth century, England marvelled and proudly beat its breast at the victories of one of its warriors. That great warrior, who knew no defeat, strung the flowers of victory into a garland and delighted in placing it around the neck of his queen, Anne. She delighted him by rewarding him with her affection. He was the Duke of Marlborough.

Winston Churchill is a direct descendant of the Marlborough line. It is a long lineage handed down from generation to generation, a family praised and celebrated by successive monarchs.

But Winston Churchill did not rest content with pride in his ancestry and waste his time. In a manner that added lustre even to the Duke of Marlborough's fame, he crushed the enemy, won the victor's wreath, and sounded the drum of triumph in the Second World War. Though he had only a few fighter aircraft at his disposal, he made thunder resound through the skies and saved the country.

Yet when the war ended in 1945, the reward the British people gave him was stranger than his victory in war:

"Thank you for the help you have rendered. It is enough that you have laboured night and day for five years to conduct the war. You need not continue to exhaust yourself by carrying the burden of the country's premiership. Go home and rest. Farewell."

This was the offering made by the British public to the prime minister who had saved the country at a time when the world trembled in fear that Britain, unable even to bear the weight of Hitler's bombs, might sink beneath the Atlantic Ocean.

Labour leader Attlee became prime minister. Churchill left office. This happened six years ago.

He did not abuse the voters as ungrateful wastrels, fools, or arrogant people incapable of distinguishing good from evil, and then retire to his home.

He went back to the voters. He explained what his party had accomplished. Again and again, without pause, he told the electorate what he intended to do if he returned to office.

In the general election held in Britain last year, he came very close to the citadel of victory. Only a shortage of a few seats prevented the Conservatives from forming a ministry.

Now Churchill has once again taken the prime minister's chair.

Churchill's victory must be called a defeat for Britain's progressive forces. Labour's radical national programmes will now be abandoned. Expenditure for the welfare of the middle and poorer classes will be cut. Private capitalists will gain the upper hand.

This raises a question: did the British public elect Churchill while knowing all this? Yes, they did so knowingly, in what they considered their own interest.

America will provide aid only if the Conservative Party governs. Britain can prosper again only if American dollars continue to pour in without obstruction for at least a little longer.

Because the British public understood this subtle calculation, they have now given the victorious warrior the office they denied him then.

It will not be long before the wheel turns once more!

(*Dravida Nadu*, 4 November 1951)
